//@version=6
// BTC ATTACK 54 - FUNDING-SETTLEMENT SQUEEZE REVERSION. LONG LEG (fades an oversized DOWN bar at a
// funding-settlement timestamp).
//
// THE STORED PROMPT still asks for Attack 37's filter stack (an EIGHTH time now), describing a board
// state (Attack 37, 322/196 trades, "earned a filter stack, queued not built") that is 17 attacks
// stale. THE DOCS OVERRIDE IT, again, per STRATEGY-LEDGER's own instruction to say so when they
// disagree: Attack 41 closed Attack 37 as a development target after four single-term filter
// rejections; Attack 43 closed the whole sweep-reversal FAMILY (37-43) on both timeframes; Attack 47 --
// the one filter tried on the current champion, Attack 46 -- died on the ~77% sample wall (HARD LESSON
// 45/49, confirmed on two unrelated mechanisms in two labs). The board's own queue after Attacks 47-53
// says stop filtering Attack 46 on this data and propose a genuinely new mechanism instead, so this
// cycle builds one, per the mandate's own fallback clause -- and per Attack 53's own queue item 4, this
// is tested on BOTH halves in one pair, without a prior single-half detour, since the mechanism-space
// search is narrowing (four straight new-mechanism proposals -- 50, 51, 52, 53 -- have now failed).
//
// ==================== WHAT HAS ALREADY BEEN TRIED, SO THIS DOES NOT REPEAT IT ====================
// Price structure alone (x7 families: VWAP, channel breakout, weekly/daily calendar anchor,
// volatility-coil, sweep-reversal 37-43, level-target tap-and-hold 44-47, round-number grid 48), a
// single bar's own statistics (Attack 49, momentum continuation), a session RANGE used as a breakout
// level (Attack 50, Asian range), a derived PRICE oscillator (Attack 51/52, RSI divergence), and a
// volume-delta order-flow proxy (Attack 53) have all been tried as PRIMARY signals. **None has used a
// CLOCK TIMESTAMP tied to a real BTC-perpetual structural mechanism as the trigger** -- Attack 50 used
// a session's price RANGE (Asia low-volume hours) as a breakout level, which is a price-structure claim
// wearing a time label; it says nothing about WHY that specific moment should behave differently. This
// build's trigger is the funding-settlement CLOCK ITSELF, not a range it happens to produce.
//
// ==================== THE CLAIM ====================
// Bybit BTCUSDT perpetuals settle funding three times a day, at 00:00 / 08:00 / 16:00 UTC. Funding is
// paid by whichever side (longs or shorts) is crowded, to the other side, and settlement is a genuine
// mechanical, calendar-fixed event independent of any price level. When the 15m bar that OPENS exactly
// at a settlement timestamp also prints an outsized DOWN move (an elevated true range relative to its
// own recent average, closing below its open), that move is more likely to be leveraged, forced
// deleveraging flow synchronised to the settlement clock than information-driven selling -- and
// mechanical flow reverts once the settlement pressure clears, unlike a genuinely information-driven
// decline. This is a claim about WHEN a move happened relative to a real exchange mechanism, not about
// its price level (distinct from Attack 37-47) and not about who was transacting via a derived
// volume-delta proxy (distinct from Attack 53).
//
// ==================== MECHANICS, NO ARRAYS, NO UDFs, NO REQUEST.SECURITY ====================
// `hour()` / `minute()` are UNIMPLEMENTED on this engine (established in Attack 50's own header) --
// UTC hour and minute are recovered by pure integer arithmetic on the `time` builtin, exactly as Attack
// 50 did: hourUTC = math.floor(time / 3600000) % 24; minuteUTC = math.floor(time / 60000) % 60.
// isSettlement flags the bar whose OPEN lands exactly on a funding timestamp (hourUTC % 8 == 0 and
// minuteUTC == 0) -- true on 3 of every 96 bars on 15m, a fixed 1-in-32 clock fact, independent of any
// price condition. avgRange is the 20-bar SMA of ta.tr, read ONE BAR BACK ([1]) so the elevated-range
// test never compares a bar's range against an average that already includes itself. rangeBig is this
// bar's own ta.tr against rangeMult times that trailing average. isBigDown is close < open on the same
// bar. All three conditions are independent (a clock fact, a volatility-relative-to-recent-history
// fact, and a same-bar direction fact) and ALL must hold on ONE bar to fire -- this is a same-bar
// trigger, not a two-stage latch (see LATCH note below).
//
// TARGET IS A LEVEL, NOT A MULTIPLE (HARD LESSON 41/47): tpPx = close[preBars], the close price traded
// `preBars` bars (1 hour at preBars=4 on 15m) BEFORE the settlement bar -- a real, already-traded price
// from immediately before the squeeze began, not a multiple of the stop. Because the squeeze is by
// definition a departure from that pre-squeeze level, the target sits on the correct side of entry by
// construction whenever the claim is right (checked explicitly via rrOk, not assumed). STOP is the
// settlement bar's OWN LOW (structure, LESSON 5) -- the extreme the forced-flow move actually printed,
// not an ATR offset and not the reclaim/target level.
//
// ==================== AUDIT, ONE LINE PER LEG (LONG ONLY -- LESSON 6) ====================
//   R >= 0.8% (LESSON 3) -- enforced by EXCLUSION via rFloorOk, measured close-to-low on the settlement
//        bar. A squeeze bar too shallow to clear the floor is SKIPPED, never widened to fit.
//   Stop beyond STRUCTURE (LESSON 5) -- slPx = the settlement bar's own low, the actual extreme printed
//        by the forced-flow move, never the entry level itself and never an ATR offset.
//   Each leg separately (LESSON 6) -- LONG ONLY this cycle, fading an oversized DOWN settlement bar.
//        The mirror (fading an oversized UP settlement bar, short) is NOT assumed identical -- HARD
//        LESSON 42/43 established a 100%-equity short is a harness margin artifact, not a signal, and
//        needs its own declared 25%-equity run that cannot sit beside this long's 100%-equity numbers
//        in the same table. Unlike a price-structure filter (support/resistance is NOT symmetric per
//        HARD LESSON 48), funding-settlement mechanics ARE directionally symmetric by construction --
//        crowded longs and crowded shorts pay the same mechanism in mirrored form -- so the short is a
//        deliberate, low-risk next step if this long clears the kill rule, built and measured on its
//        own, not carried over from this run. Its absence here is a declared scope limit, not a design
//        claim that it works.
//   BINDING (E17) -- THREE independent conditions must all be true on the SAME bar (settlement clock,
//        elevated range, down close); trade count is read before any ratio is quoted (LESSON 12).
//   REDUNDANCY (E14) -- rFloorOk constrains the STOP distance (close-to-low, an absolute % floor);
//        rrOk only checks tpPx sits above close (existence, not a size threshold). Non-overlapping,
//        same pattern as Attack 44/50/51/52/53.
//   LATCH IN SEQUENCE (LESSON 8) -- N/A BY DESIGN, STATED EXPLICITLY: all three trigger conditions
//        (clock, range, direction) are evaluated and satisfied on ONE bar, with no separate arm stage
//        to sequence -- the same accepted same-bar-trigger shape as Attack 48 (round-number cross) and
//        Attack 49 (impulse-bar continuation), not a violation of the two-stage-latch requirement,
//        which applies only when a design HAS an arm/trigger split.
//   SL and TP FIXED AT ENTRY (slPx/tpPx captured once, at the entry bar, from the settlement bar's own
//        low and the preBars-back close). No trailing, no averaging down, no martingale.
//   CASCADE (HARD LESSON 35/50) -- long build at 100% equity with a fixed stop/target; expect
//        cascadeRatio 1, verified via get_trades before applying RATCHET v2 clause 3 if this advances.
//   WEEKENDS (structural note 4) -- funding settles identically on weekends; no special-casing, stated
//        rather than silently assumed. The settlement clock does not distinguish weekday from weekend.
//
// ==================== FREQUENCY ESTIMATE, A HYPOTHESIS TO BE SCORED (HARD LESSON 4) ====================
// ~163,700 15m bars over the backtest window; settlement bars are exactly 1-in-32 (~5,115 bars). Down
// bars are ~50% of those. An elevated-range bar (>=1.5x the trailing 20-bar average true range) is
// estimated at roughly 20% of bars given typical right-skewed TR distributions on this asset. Naive
// conjunction: 5,115 * 0.50 * 0.20 ~ 510 candidate bars before the R-floor exclusion, split roughly by
// bar-count share across the two halves (~85,655 H1 bars vs ~78,000 H2 bars per Attack 43's own count)
// to somewhere near 270 / 240 before the floor trims further. Read the ACTUAL count from the result,
// never trust this estimate (HARD LESSON 4) -- if it misses by more than 2x, say so explicitly.
//
// ==================== OUTCOMES REGISTERED BEFORE THE RUN (LESSON 17) ====================
//   * PRE-2024 HALF (H1, never-tuned, 2022-01-01 -> 2024-06-08) BELOW 1.0 -> KILL RULE. Discard, do NOT
//     run H2, do NOT add filters to rescue it -- this is a bare mechanism test.
//   * BOTH HALVES >= 1.0 -> a third genuinely-new, both-halves-positive family alongside Attack 37
//     (closed on cost) and Attack 46 (the current champion) -- queues for its own cold re-run and cost
//     screen (HARD LESSON 25/37) before any filter-stack treatment, per the mandate's own logic.
//   * EITHER HALF UNDER ~30 TRADES -> that half's ratio is a DIRECTION, not a result (LESSON 12); the
//     three-way conjunction is expected to bind hard (see BINDING above), so a thin count is a real,
//     registered-in-advance risk, not a surprise to explain away afterward.
//   * TRADE COUNT FAR ABOVE THE ~510 ESTIMATE (>2x) -> the elevated-range assumption was wrong, a
//     frequency finding in its own right per HARD LESSON 4, reported before interpreting the PF.
//   * A MAJORITY OF HOLDS AT maxBars 192 -> HARD LESSON 38 truncation, reported before any conclusion
//     is drawn from the achieved win/loss ratio.
//   REPORT gross, commission, achieved ratio, avg loser, win rate and max drawdown together, per the
//   board's standing instruction since Attack 37 named the three drawdown categories (HARD LESSON
//   36/39).

strategy("BTC Attack54 funding settlement reversion", overlay=true,
     pyramiding=1, process_orders_on_close=true,
     commission_type=strategy.commission.percent, commission_value=0.05,
     initial_capital=10000,
     default_qty_type=strategy.percent_of_equity, default_qty_value=100,
     margin_long=100, margin_short=100)

rangeMult = input.float(1.5,  "Elevated-range multiple of trailing 20-bar avg true range")
avgLook   = input.int(20,    "Lookback for the trailing average true range")
preBars   = input.int(4,     "Bars before the settlement bar used as the target anchor (4 = 1h on 15m)")
minRpct   = input.float(0.80, "Min stop distance as % of price (LESSON 3)")
maxBars   = input.int(192,   "Max bars in trade")

hourUTC   = math.floor(time / 3600000) % 24     // hour() is unimplemented on this engine (Attack 50)
minuteUTC = math.floor(time / 60000) % 60

isSettlement = (hourUTC % 8 == 0) and (minuteUTC == 0)

avgRange = ta.sma(ta.tr, avgLook)[1]
rangeBig = not na(avgRange) and ta.tr >= rangeMult * avgRange
isBigDown = close < open

signal = isSettlement and rangeBig and isBigDown

flat = strategy.position_size == 0

slCand = low
tpCand = close[preBars]

rLong    = close - slCand
minR     = close * minRpct / 100
rFloorOk = rLong >= minR
rrOk     = not na(tpCand) and tpCand > close

goLong = flat and signal and rFloorOk and rrOk

var float slPx   = na
var float tpPx   = na
var int   barsIn = 0

if flat and goLong
    strategy.entry("L", strategy.long)
    slPx   := slCand
    tpPx   := tpCand
    barsIn := 0

if strategy.position_size > 0
    strategy.exit("LX", from_entry="L", stop=slPx, limit=tpPx)
    barsIn := barsIn + 1

if barsIn >= maxBars and strategy.position_size != 0
    strategy.close_all()
    barsIn := 0

plot(goLong ? 1 : 0, title="LongSignal")
plot(slPx, title="StopPx")
plot(tpPx, title="TargetPx")
